5. Design patterns and OOP principles
Principle or pattern	Where it is used
Strategy pattern	EvictionPolicy with LRUEvictionPolicy
Programming to an interface	Cache depends on Storage and EvictionPolicy
Single Responsibility Principle	Storage manages data; policy manages eviction
Open/Closed Principle	New eviction policies can be added without rewriting Cache
Dependency injection	Policy and storage are passed through the Cache constructor
Encapsulation	Internal map and linked-list state are hidden inside their classes

The Strategy pattern is especially important here: the cache does not need to know how LRU works. It simply calls keyAccessed() and evictKey().
6. Time and space complexity
Operation	Average time
Storage get()	\(O(1)\)
Storage add()	\(O(1)\)
Storage remove()	\(O(1)\)
LRU keyAccessed()	\(O(1)\)
LRU evictKey()	\(O(1)\)
Cache get()	\(O(1)\)
Cache put()	\(O(1)\) average

Space complexity is \(O(C)\), where \(C\) is the cache capacity. The cache stores up to \(C\) entries and the LRU policy maintains up to \(C\) list nodes and map entries.

These complexities assume average-case HashMap operations.

