This code implements an LRU Cache (Least Recently Used Cache) in Java using a HashMap, a custom Doubly Linked List, and several object-oriented design principles.

I’ll explain it class by class, then dry-run the main() method so you understand exactly how the cache works.
1. What is an LRU Cache?
An LRU cache stores a limited number of key-value pairs. When the cache becomes full and a new entry needs to be inserted, it removes the least recently used entry.

For example, suppose the cache capacity is 3.
Operation	Cache entries, oldest → newest
put(A, Apple)	A
put(B, Banana)	A → B
put(C, Cherry)	A → B → C
get(A)	B → C → A
put(D, Dates)	C → A → D

When we access A, it becomes the most recently used entry. Therefore, when D is inserted, B is removed, because B is the least recently used.

The code uses two data structures to achieve efficient operations:
- HashMap: Finds a key's linked-list node in \(O(1)\) average time.
- Doubly Linked List: Moves and removes nodes in \(O(1)\) time when the node reference is known.
