These two references keep track of the ends of the list.
- first: the oldest, least recently used key.
- last: the newest, most recently used key.
For this implementation, the list is ordered from LRU to MRU.
Example: A → B → C
A

LRU

B

C

MRU


first → A

last → C


A. Constructor
public DoublyLinkedList() {
    first = null;
    last = null;
}


When the list is empty, neither end points to a node.
B. addElementAtLast(E element)
public DoublyLinkedListNode<E> addElementAtLast(E element) {
    DoublyLinkedListNode<E> node =
        new DoublyLinkedListNode<>(element);

    if (last == null) {
        first = last = node;
    } else {
        last.next = node;
        node.prev = last;
        last = node;
    }

    return node;
}


Purpose: Add an element to the MRU end and return its node reference.

There are two cases.

Case 1: The list is empty
first = last = node;


Both first and last point to the newly created node.

Case 2: The list already contains nodes
last.next = node;
node.prev = last;
last = node;


Suppose the list is A → B and we add C.
1. last.next = node: B's next points to C.
2. node.prev = last: C's prev points to B.
3. last = node: last now points to C.
Result: A ⇄ B ⇄ C.

Why return node? The LRU policy stores this reference in a HashMap so that it can find and move the node later without searching the list.
C. detachNode(node)
This method removes a node from any position in the list.
public void detachNode(DoublyLinkedListNode<E> node) {
    if (node == null) {
        return;
    }

    if (node == first) {
        first = node.next;

        if (first != null) {
            first.prev = null;
        }
    } else {
        node.prev.next = node.next;
    }

    if (node == last) {
        last = node.prev;

        if (last != null) {
            last.next = null;
        }
    } else {
        node.next.prev = node.prev;
    }

    node.prev = null;
    node.next = null;
}


This method handles four important situations.
First

Remove the first node

For A ⇄ B ⇄ C, removing A makes B the new first. B's prev becomes null.



Middle

Remove a middle node

Removing B connects A directly to C: A ⇄ C.



Last

Remove the last node

Removing C makes B the new last. B's next becomes null.



Null

Null node

The method returns immediately without modifying the list.



Finally:
node.prev = null;
node.next = null;


Disconnects the removed node from the list.

Important: This implementation assumes that a non-null node belongs to the list and that the list's links are valid. Passing an unrelated node could cause a NullPointerException or corrupt the list.
D. getFirst() and isEmpty()
public DoublyLinkedListNode<E> getFirst() {
    return first;
}

public boolean isEmpty() {
    return first == null;
}


- getFirst() returns the first node, which is the LRU node.
- isEmpty() returns true if the list contains no nodes.
The eviction policy uses getFirst() to identify which key to remove.
4. LRUEvictionPolicy<K> — decides which key to evict
This class implements the EvictionPolicy<K> interface.
private final DoublyLinkedList<K> dll;
private final Map<K, DoublyLinkedListNode<K>> mapper;


The two fields work together:
- dll: stores keys in usage order.
- mapper: maps each key to its corresponding node.
For example:
Doubly linked list
